---
/**
 * Layer 1 of the birth-time funnel: the public, free, evergreen resource.
 *
 * URL is `/birth-time/` rather than `/how-to-find-your-birth-time/`. The site's
 * convention for durable hub pages is a short noun path — /start-here/,
 * /horoscopes/, /credentials/, /approach/ — with the descriptive phrasing
 * carried by the title and h1 where it actually helps a searcher. A shorter
 * path also survives the page growing beyond "how to find" into what to do when
 * you cannot.
 *
 * Nothing here is gated. Birth time is the single most common reason someone
 * decides not to book, so the page that answers it completely is worth more in
 * search — and more to the person reading it — than a teaser would be in
 * signups. The toolkit is offered as a working process for people who have
 * read this and now need to go and do it.
 *
 * Written from approved material already on the site (the two birth-time blog
 * posts, /prepare-for-your-reading/, the FAQs) plus researched record-retrieval
 * guidance. Three things are kept deliberately separate throughout: what is a
 * matter of record-keeping fact, what is astrological technique, and what is
 * Mo's own judgement. Where jurisdictions vary the page says so rather than
 * describing one country's process as if it were universal.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
import { scenes } from '../config/images';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { defaultBookingHref } from '../config/booking.ts';

const crumbs = [{ label: 'Find your birth time', href: '/birth-time/' }];
const description =
  'Where a birth time is recorded, how to request it, what to ask relatives, and how much precision actually matters. Plus what a reading can still do when the time is uncertain.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'How to find your birth time',
    description,
    mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': new URL('/birth-time/', site.url).href },
    author: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: 'Maureen "Mo" Lumen',
      url: new URL('/about/', site.url).href,
    },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: site.name, url: site.url },
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    about: [
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Birth time' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Ascendant' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Natal chart' },
    ],
  },
  /**
   * FAQPage on four questions that are genuinely asked and genuinely answered
   * on the page — not markup invented to win a rich result. Each answer is the
   * same answer a reader gets in the body; if one is ever edited, edit both.
   */
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How accurate does a birth time need to be for astrology?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'It depends on where the chart falls, not on a fixed number of minutes. The rising sign changes about every two hours, so a time known to within an hour usually fixes it, unless the birth happened near a sign boundary, where a few minutes decide it. The same is true of any planet sitting close to a house cusp. A time good to fifteen minutes is enough for most of what a reading does. Precision to the minute matters mainly for techniques that divide time finely, and for charts where something important sits on an edge.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Where is a birth time officially recorded?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Most often on the long-form or full birth certificate held by the government office responsible for vital records where the birth took place. The short certificate many households keep frequently omits the time. Whether a time was recorded at all, and whether it is released on request, varies by country, state or province, and by how old the record is. Hospital medical records, baby books, family papers and newspaper birth announcements are the other places worth looking.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What if two family members remember different birth times?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Keep both. Recorded conflict is more useful than a premature decision, because two candidate times can be tested against a chart while a single guessed time cannot. Note who said what, how confident they were, and what they were anchoring the memory to. A documented time from a certificate outranks any recollection; between two recollections, the one anchored to an event usually beats the one anchored to a number.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Can you read a chart without a birth time?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Yes, with stated limits. Every planet’s sign, the aspects between them and the current transits are all available without a time. That is most of a chart, and it is where a great deal of character lives. What is unavailable is the rising sign, the house structure, and the Moon’s exact degree on a fast-moving day. A reading built on what is solid answers what these patterns are very well, and where in your life they land only in outline.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to find your birth time"
  description={description}
  path="/birth-time/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  schema={schema}
  ogType="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />

  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Free guide</p>
        <h1>How to find your birth time</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Not knowing your birth time stops more people from booking a reading than anything else.
          It is usually findable, it is rarely as catastrophic as people fear when it isn't, and
          this page is the whole of what I know about both.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.prepareReading.src}
        srcset={scenes.prepareReading.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.prepareReading.alt}
        width={scenes.prepareReading.width}
        height={scenes.prepareReading.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow" style="max-width: 42rem;">
      {/*
        Concise-answer passages under descriptive headings, throughout. The
        heading states the question a searcher typed and the first sentence
        answers it, so the page is quotable by an answer engine without the
        reader having to hunt. The nuance follows the answer rather than
        replacing it.
      */}
      <h2>What a birth time actually changes</h2>
      <p>
        The birth time sets the <a href="/explore/angles/">rising sign</a> and, with it, the whole
        <a href="/explore/houses/">house structure</a> of the chart. Your Sun moves about a degree a
        day and takes a month to cross a sign. The rising degree crosses the entire zodiac in
        twenty-four hours, so a new sign comes up roughly every two hours. One of those is a season.
        The other is a snapshot.
      </p>
      <p>
        That matters beyond having one more sign to talk about. The houses are the twelve areas of
        life the chart divides into, and where they begin depends on the rising degree. Move it far
        enough and every planet changes which area of life it is operating in, Saturn shifts from
        your house of career to your house of partnership, and those are different conversations.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it does <em>not</em> mean is that a few minutes always changes everything. That claim
        gets repeated a lot and it is only sometimes true. Which brings us to the question people
        actually want answered.
      </p>

      <h2>How accurate does it need to be?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>It depends on where your chart falls, not on a fixed number of minutes.</strong> A
        time known to within an hour usually settles the rising sign, and a time good to about
        fifteen minutes is enough for most of what a reading does.
      </p>
      <p>
        The exceptions are the cases that make the alarming version of this advice true. If your
        rising degree sits near the boundary between two signs, a few minutes decides which one it
        is, and that is a genuinely different chart. The same applies to any planet sitting close to
        a house cusp: a small shift moves it across a line. And techniques that divide time finely
, some predictive methods advance the chart by a degree or so per year of life, will
        magnify a small error into a noticeable one.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the honest answer is that precision matters enormously in some charts and hardly at all
        in others, and you cannot tell which you have until someone looks. That is a reason to hunt
        for the time, and not a reason to panic about the last four minutes of it.
      </p>

      <h2>Start with the records you already have</h2>
      <p>
        Before requesting anything, check what is already in the house. Time of birth is the sort of
        detail written down once, in the moment, and then filed.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your birth certificate</strong>, but check which one you have. Many households
          keep an abbreviated or wallet-sized certificate that omits the time. The full or long-form
          version is the one worth finding.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A baby book or hospital keepsake.</strong> Often recorded to the minute, and often
          copied straight off the hospital paperwork.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Family papers.</strong> A parent's diary, a family bible, a letter written that
          week, the back of a photograph, an old address book.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Newspaper birth announcements.</strong> Local papers historically printed birth
          notices, sometimes with times. Library and newspaper archives have found times for people
          who had given up.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Requesting an official record</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Birth records are held by whichever authority registers births where you were
        born</strong>: typically a national, state or provincial vital records office, sometimes
        with a county or municipal counterpart that holds the local copy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Beyond that, the specifics genuinely vary, and anyone who tells you otherwise is describing
        their own country. What differs from place to place:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Whether the time was recorded at all.</strong> Some jurisdictions have never
          captured time of birth on the certificate. Older records are less consistent than recent
          ones.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Whether the time appears on the copy you can obtain.</strong> Some offices issue
          an abbreviated certificate by default and a full copy only on request, sometimes only to
          the person named or an immediate relative.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What identification and fee are required</strong>, and how long it takes.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The practical move is to search for the vital records office for the place of birth, look
        for wording like "long form", "full certificate" or "certified copy with all details", and
        read what that particular office says before paying for anything. If the time is not on the
        certificate, the hospital's own medical records department is the next call, some retain
        birth records for decades, and their release process is separate from the registry's.
      </p>
      <p class="card__meta">
        This is guidance on where to look, not legal advice, and it is not specific to your
        jurisdiction. The office that holds your record is the authority on its own process.
      </p>

      <h2>Asking your family well</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Ask about the shape of the day rather than the number on the clock.</strong> People
        remember events far better than they remember times, and "what time was I born?" invites a
        rounded guess that then hardens into a fact.
      </p>
      <p>Better prompts:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Was it light or dark outside?</li>
        <li>Before or after a meal, which one?</li>
        <li>Had Dad already left for work, or come home?</li>
        <li>Did anyone miss something because of it: a shift, a class, a service?</li>
        <li>Who else was there, and had they arrived yet or come later?</li>
        <li>Was it the same day labour started?</li>
        <li>Did the hospital staff change over while you were there?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Answers like these narrow a twenty-four-hour window to a few hours, which is real progress.
        Treat them as memory aids rather than evidence: a recollection, however confident, is not
        the same kind of thing as a line on a certificate, and it is worth writing down which one
        you have.
      </p>

      <h2>When the records disagree</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Keep both times rather than picking one.</strong> A recorded conflict is more useful
        than a premature decision, because two candidate times can be tested against a chart while a
        single guessed time cannot be tested against anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        Write down each candidate, where it came from, and how confident that source is. A
        documented time outranks any recollection. Between two recollections, the one anchored to an
        event usually beats the one anchored to a number. If two candidates produce the same rising
        sign, the disagreement may not matter at all, and that is worth knowing before anyone
        spends effort resolving it.
      </p>

      <h2>If the time cannot be found</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>You can still have a real reading.</strong> It will be a different reading, and any
        astrologer worth booking will tell you which parts are affected rather than quietly guessing
        at them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Unavailable without a time:</strong> the rising sign, the house structure, and the
        Moon's exact degree. The Moon moves twelve to thirteen degrees a day, so across an unknown
        day it can shift meaningfully and occasionally change sign.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Unaffected:</strong> every planet's sign, in almost all cases. The aspects between
        them, which is the geometry of the chart and where a great deal of character lives. The
        condition of each planet by <a href="/explore/dignities/">dignity</a>. The current transits
        and which natal placements they are contacting.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is most of a chart. A reading built on it answers <em>what are these patterns</em> very
        well, and <em>where in your life do they land</em> only in outline. It is not a consolation
        prize.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one thing genuinely worth avoiding is guessing and presenting the guess as exact. An
        honest "I don't know" produces a good reading with stated limits. A confident wrong time
        produces a detailed, coherent, wholly inapplicable reading about someone born two hours
        after you, and neither of you finds out until you are partway through wondering why none of
        it fits.
      </p>

      <h2>What rectification is, and is not</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Rectification is an astrological technique for narrowing a probable birth time by
        working backwards from known life events.</strong> It is a real discipline and practitioners
        who specialise in it do careful, skilled work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two qualifications matter. It is substantial work and priced accordingly. And it produces a
        well-reasoned hypothesis that fits the events you supplied, not a recovered fact, and not
        verification in any external sense. Different rectifiers can reach different answers from
        the same life. If someone offers you a rectified time with certainty, that confidence is not
        warranted by the method.
      </p>
      <p>
        For most people the full birth certificate is the better first spend.
      </p>

      <h2>What to bring to a reading</h2>
      <p>
        Send the birth date, the birth city and country, and the time <em>with your confidence
        attached</em>. That last part is the one people leave off and the one that changes how the
        chart gets read: "from the certificate", "between 2 and 4pm", "sometime in the morning" and
        "genuinely no idea" each lead somewhere different, and all four are workable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not sure which of those you have? <a href="/birth-time-toolkit/#confidence-check">Three
        questions will tell you</a>. It gives back one of five plain labels and the next thing
        worth doing about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/prepare-for-your-reading/">How to prepare for your reading</a> covers everything
        else worth bringing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/*
    The toolkit offer sits here, after the reader has the answer — not before it
    and not interrupting it. Someone who has read this far knows what they are
    looking for and now has to go and do it, which is exactly the moment a
    working process is worth having.
  */}
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container feature-split">
      <div class="flow">
        <p class="eyebrow">The next step</p>
        <h2>Working with an uncertain birth time?</h2>
        <p>
          This page is what I know. The <strong>Birth Time Toolkit</strong> is how to use it: a
          worksheet set that walks the search in order: what you already know, which records to
          request, what to ask each relative, how to compare times that disagree, and how to write
          the result up so an astrologer knows exactly what they are working with.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's free with the monthly sky letter, and there's a plain download link if you'd rather
          not subscribe.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="btn btn--accent" href="/birth-time-toolkit/" data-toolkit-cta="birth-time-article">
            Get the Birth Time Toolkit
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        src={scenes.newsletterLetters.src}
        alt={scenes.newsletterLetters.alt}
        width={scenes.newsletterLetters.width}
        height={scenes.newsletterLetters.height}
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container grid grid--3">
      <article class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
          <a href="/explore/the-big-three/">The Big Three</a>
        </h2>
        <p style="margin: 0;">What the rising sign contributes once you have it.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
          <a href="/explore/houses/">The twelve houses</a>
        </h2>
        <p style="margin: 0;">The areas of life the birth time anchors.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
          <a href="/prepare-for-your-reading/">Prepare for your reading</a>
        </h2>
        <p style="margin: 0;">Everything else worth bringing.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Found it, or found the range?"
    body="Either is workable. Bring what you have and the real question you've been carrying."
    buttonLabel="Book This Reading"
    buttonHref={defaultBookingHref()}
  />
</BaseLayout>
